---
/**
 * SupportHero — overrides Starlight's `Hero` on the support landing page.
 *
 * WHY AN OVERRIDE AND NOT A COMPONENT IN THE MDX: Starlight's own Hero sizes its h1 in a
 * COMPONENT-SCOPED stylesheet, so the brand bridge's `--sl-text-h1: 32px` never reached it and the
 * title rendered at 64px — a variable map repaints Starlight but cannot re-proportion it. Replacing
 * the component is the only way to reach geometry that lives in scoped CSS. This closes open item 3
 * of the support-portal list at the same time as it builds the new landing.
 *
 * THE DARK BAND IS DELIBERATE IN BOTH THEMES. The rays asset is a cyan burst FLATTENED ONTO BLACK
 * and composited with `mix-blend-mode: screen`; screen(a, white) === white, so on the light theme's
 * near-white floor (L 0.9765) it renders exactly nothing. Rather than ship a second asset that has
 * to be kept in sync and is always the weaker of the two, the band carries its own dark ground in
 * both themes (Oleh's call, 2026-08-18) — so the person reviewing in light mode sees the same front
 * door as everyone else. `data-theme="dark"` is what supplies the palette: the brand bridge's dark
 * block is written with NON-`:root` selectors too, so the whole `--bo-*` / `--sl-*` set resolves to
 * its dark values inside this subtree. That is why there is not one raw hex below.
 *
 * THE FIELD IS A TRIGGER, NOT AN INPUT (Oleh, 2026-08-18). It used to be a real input with its own
 * results list, which was a second implementation of the ⌘K modal — a second set of keyboard rules,
 * a second empty state, and a dropdown that had to fight the band's own clipping to be seen. It now
 * opens that modal, so the portal has ONE search surface and the landing gets the resting state
 * (recently viewed · suggested · ask) for free. `lib/ask.ts` was deleted with it.
 *
 * THE PLATFORM CONTROL IS ABOVE THE FIELD, NOT INSIDE IT (Oleh, 2026-08-21). The search modal has
 * carried a `PlatformPicker` since it was built, and that is the diagnosis: the control that SCOPES
 * the search was hidden inside the thing it scopes, so a reader looking straight at a search box
 * had no way of seeing that a hidden element was narrowing every result it would return. In his
 * words: the user sees the search bar in front of them and must SEE, with their eyes, the options
 * for where they back up — and the content below follows the choice.
 *
 * SO IT SITS IN THE BAND, DIRECTLY ABOVE THE FIELD, and it is the same shared control (`state`
 * defaults to `shared`), writing the one `bo-platforms` preference the sidebar, the modal, the chat
 * and the strip below all read. Nothing here is a second copy of that state: this is a third PLACE
 * to set the one value, and setting it here repaints the rest live through `FILTER_EVENT`.
 *
 * IT DOES NOT REPLACE THE MODAL'S OWN PICKER, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight. Every
 * page but this one has no hero, and ⌘K opens the modal from all of them; more to the point, while
 * the dialog is open it is in the top layer and this control is behind a backdrop and unreachable,
 * so a filter carried into the dialog would silently remove results with nothing on screen saying
 * why. Two places, one value, and never both reachable at once.
 *
 * IT DOES NOT REPLACE THE PLATFORM CARDS BELOW EITHER: those are DOORS. This says "these are the
 * platforms I care about" and scopes; a card says "take me there now" and narrows on the way out.
 * Different acts, and the copy of each is written so the page is not asking one question twice.
 *
 * SEARCH AND ASK SIT TOGETHER. The spec's funnel is docs first (cheapest — nobody's time is
 * spent), chat when the docs failed. The escape hatch therefore belongs INSIDE the result list, at
 * the moment of failure, rather than as a second control the visitor must notice and choose between.
 * The Ask row is always the first option, so it is also the answer when there are no results at all.
 */
import { Icon } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import { POPULAR_QUESTIONS } from '../lib/questions';
import PlatformPicker from './PlatformPicker.astro';

const hero = Astro.locals.starlightRoute.entry.data.hero;
const title = hero?.title ?? Astro.locals.starlightRoute.entry.data.title;
const tagline = hero?.tagline;
---

<section class="sh" data-theme="dark" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <div class="sh-rays" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="sh-inner">
    <h1 id="_top" class="sh-title">{title}</h1>
    {tagline && <p class="sh-tagline" set:html={tagline} />}

    {/* THE LABEL IS A QUESTION AND IT IS VISIBLE, because the whole defect being fixed here is a
        scoping control nobody could see. `PlatformPicker`'s own `label` is the accessible name;
        this is the sighted reader's half of the same sentence. */}
    <div class="sh-plat">
      <span class="sh-plat-label">Which platforms do you back up?</span>
      <PlatformPicker label="Which platforms do you back up" />
    </div>

    <div class="sh-ask">
      <button type="button" class="sh-field" data-search-open>
        <span class="sh-field-icon" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="magnifier" /></span>
        <span class="sh-field-text">Search the docs</span>
        <kbd class="sh-kbd">⌘K</kbd>
      </button>

      <button type="button" class="sh-ask-btn" data-chat-open aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="sh-ask-btn-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M9.937 15.5A2 2 0 0 0 8.5 14.063l-6.135-1.582a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.962L8.5 9.936A2 2 0 0 0 9.937 8.5l1.582-6.135a.5.5 0 0 1 .963 0L14.063 8.5A2 2 0 0 0 15.5 9.937l6.135 1.581a.5.5 0 0 1 0 .964L15.5 14.063a2 2 0 0 0-1.437 1.437l-1.582 6.135a.5.5 0 0 1-.963 0z" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        Ask AI
      </button>
    </div>

    {/* Directly under the field, inside the band: these ARE the search, pre-typed. Below the fold
        in a section of their own they read as an afterthought; here they answer the question the
        heading just asked. */}
    <ul class="sh-chips">
      {POPULAR_QUESTIONS.map((q) => (
        <li><a href={q.href}>{q.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>

  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Lets the band run past this column's edges without the PAGE scrolling sideways.
     `overflow-x: clip` and NOT `container-type: inline-size`, which was the first fix here:
     `container-type` establishes containment, containment makes the element a containing block for
     `position: fixed` descendants, and that would pin `ChatDock`'s drawer to `main` instead of the
     viewport. `clip` bounds the overflow without creating a containing block, so the fixed drawer
     still escapes it. `:global` because it is Starlight's element; safe because this stylesheet
     ships only on pages that render a Hero — today, the landing alone. */
  :global(main) {
    overflow-x: clip;
  }

  /* The band owns its ground in both themes — see the header note. `--bo-*` resolve dark here
     because of `data-theme="dark"` on the section. */
  .sh {
    position: relative;
    /* NO `overflow: hidden` here. It was clipping the results panel, which drops out of the band by
       design — measured: the list was cut off mid-row at the band's lower edge. The rays layer is
       `inset: 0` of this element and cannot spill on its own, and the full-bleed's own overshoot is
       already absorbed by `overflow-x: clip` on `main`. */

    /* THE THREE MUTED GREYS MUST BE RE-DECLARED HERE, and this is not belt-and-braces.
       The bridge defines them in a plain `:root` block as `oklch(from var(--bo-base-content) …)`.
       A custom property is computed AT THE ELEMENT THAT DECLARES IT, so those resolve once against
       `:root`'s base-content — near-black — and then inherit down as a finished colour. Redefining
       `--bo-base-content` in a NESTED theme scope cannot reach back and re-derive them. The site's
       own dark theme is unaffected because there `data-theme` sits on `<html>`, which IS `:root`.
       Measured before this fix: the tagline, the search icon and the placeholder all computed to
       `oklch(0.1776 … / .85)` — black text on a black band. Values copied from the bridge's own
       ladder, including its rule that `.35` is ornament-only and anything READ takes `.65`. */
    --bo-muted: oklch(from var(--bo-base-content) l c h / 0.65);
    --bo-muted-strong: oklch(from var(--bo-base-content) l c h / 0.85);
    --bo-ornament: oklch(from var(--bo-base-content) l c h / 0.35);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bo-root-bg) 0%, var(--bo-base-100) 50%, var(--bo-root-bg) 100%);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    /* FULL-BLEED OUT OF STARLIGHT'S SPLASH COLUMN. `vw` counts the classic scrollbar, so this
       overshoots by half its width at each edge — measured scrollWidth 1432 against clientWidth
       1425, hero left −7, the whole page scrolling sideways. The overshoot is absorbed by
       `overflow-x: clip` on `main` above rather than by measuring differently, because the
       alternative (a container query) would trap the chat drawer. */
    margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw);
    padding-inline: calc(50vw - 50%);
  }

  /* Same treatment as the product's auth screens (`apps/web/src/styles/components/auth.css`).
     `screen` is load-bearing: the asset is flattened onto black, so a black pixel contributes
     nothing and the alpha channel is mathematically redundant. Do not add an opacity multiplier —
     the art already peaks at 73/255 and would disappear. */
  .sh-rays {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background-image: url('/images/auth-rays.webp');
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: top center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .sh-inner {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* Taller now that the questions live here — the band has to hold two rows of chips without
       crowding the field they belong to. */
    padding-block: 72px 56px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .sh-title {
    font-size: var(--sl-text-4xl);
    line-height: 1.15;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sh-tagline {
    margin: 12px auto 0;
    max-width: 32rem;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-lg);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* ── The platform scope, and then the search it scopes ──────────────────────────────────────
     THE TWO ARE ONE GROUP, and the spacing has to say so: 32px from the tagline to the group, 12px
     inside it. Written the other way round — an equal gap above and below the picker — the control
     reads as a third thing floating between the tagline and the field rather than as the label on
     the field's scope.

     CENTRED, because the band is. The field is a full-width block, so a left-aligned row above it
     would be the only left-aligned thing in a centred column.

     `flex-wrap` IS THE 390 BEHAVIOUR, and it is the whole responsive story here: the question is
     ~29 characters and the trigger is a 36px-tall button, so below roughly 460px of measure the
     trigger drops to its own line under the question, both still centred, and the pair costs one
     extra ~48px row. Nothing is hidden and no breakpoint is written down. The trigger's own
     `max-width: 100%` (`PlatformPicker.astro`) is what keeps a narrowed state — marks plus a count
     — from pushing the band sideways at that width. */
  .sh-plat {
    margin-top: 32px;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  /* Starlight's prose rhythm margins every sibling after the first inside any flex box it reaches,
     and this row is exactly the two-child case that has already put a control 16px below its own
     label in the search modal. `:global` on the child half deliberately: `PlatformPicker` renders
     with its own scope hash, so a bare `> *` would ship as valid CSS matching nothing. */
  .sh-plat > :global(*) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .sh-plat-label {
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* THE TRIGGER NEEDS THE BAND'S VEIL, for the reason `.sh-field` has one three rules below: the
     control paints itself `--bo-base-100`, which is #111111 in dark, and the band's own gradient
     runs #0a0a0a → #111111 — so inside this section the button would be the same colour as the
     thing behind it. A tint of the text colour reads as raised over the gradient and over the rays
     alike. `:global` because the trigger is a child component's element and a scoped selector
     cannot reach it. The popover keeps its own opaque surface: it is a menu, and a translucent
     menu over a photographic band is unreadable. */
  .sh :global(.pk-trigger) {
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 7%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 14%, transparent);
  }

  .sh :global(.pk-trigger:hover) {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* ── Search + Ask ── */
  .sh-ask {
    position: relative;
    /* 12px, not 32: see `.sh-plat` — the scope control and the field it scopes are one group. */
    margin-top: 12px;
    text-align: start;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .sh-field { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }

  .sh-ask-btn {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    height: 52px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 14%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 7%, transparent);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-base);
    font-weight: 500;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .sh-ask-btn:hover { border-color: var(--bo-primary); }
  .sh-ask-btn-icon { display: flex; color: var(--bo-primary); }

  /* ── ONE ROW AT EVERY WIDTH (Oleh, 2026-08-26) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     "Так як у нас все одно відкривається модалка для пошуку, давай кнопку Ask AI вмістимо в рядок
     з інпутом серча."

     It used to stack below 40rem, and the arithmetic is why it had to: the Ask AI button is 98px at
     its natural width, and `Search the docs or ask a question` needs 219px of text — 279px of field
     once the glyph, the gap and the padding are counted. 279 + 8 + 98 = 385 against a 366px column
     at 390. Nineteen pixels short, so the row broke.

     THE NINETEEN PIXELS CAME OUT OF THE COPY, NOT THE LAYOUT. `or ask a question` is a description of
     the control sitting immediately beside it, and once the two share a line it is the caption of a
     button the reader can already see. DESIGN.md's copy rule is that every word earns its place. The
     label is `Search the docs` now, at every width — the same shortening the header pair made when
     it collapsed, arrived at from the other direction. Measured after: the row needs 266px, the
     column has 296 even at 320.

     AND THE HEIGHT BUG GOES WITH THE BLOCK. That media query is where `.sh-field` painted 30px
     instead of 52 — `flex: 1` is `flex-basis: 0%`, which sizes the MAIN axis, and the rule had just
     turned that axis vertical. It was patched with `flex: none` on 2026-08-25; the patch is gone
     with the cause, and `flex: 1` now means what it was written to mean. Re-measured: 52px at 320,
     390, 430 and 720.

     ── (the 2026-08-25 note, kept because the trap is worth finding twice) ──────────────────────
     `.sh-field` declares `height: 52px` and painted 52 on a desktop. Measured at an emulated 390:
     **30px** — the portal's single most-used control, three quarters of an inch of finger short of
     anything tappable, on the page every visitor lands on.

     Nothing overrode the height. `flex: 1` is `flex-grow:1 flex-shrink:1 flex-basis:0%`, and
     `flex-basis` sizes the MAIN axis — which this rule has just turned from horizontal to vertical.
     So below 40rem the field asked for a basis of 0 along its height, `.sh-ask` is sized by its
     content and had no free space to distribute, and the box collapsed onto its line box. Both
     declarations were correct; the direction under them changed what one of them meant.

     `flex: none` restores the height, and it is stated here rather than by raising the height
     somewhere else: the 52 is not wrong, it was never reached. The touch floor in `styles/
     support.css` would also have clamped this to 44 — a control landing on the floor rather than at
     its designed size is a symptom being treated, so the cause is fixed here and the floor stays
     what it is, a floor. Re-measured after: 52.0 at 390, 375 and 320.

     AND THE BLOCK MOVED DOWN THE FILE TO DO IT. It used to sit above the base `.sh-field` rule,
     which declares the `flex: 1` this is correcting at the same specificity — so written in place
     it would have lost on source order and computed 30 anyway, with the file reading as fixed.
     `specs/16-responsive.md` §10.1: a narrow block goes after the last base declaration of every
     selector it names. */
  .sh-field {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-base);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    cursor: pointer;
    text-align: start;
    padding: 0 16px;
    height: 52px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    /* A TRANSLUCENT VEIL, not `--bo-base-100`. That token is #111111 in dark and the band's own
       gradient runs #0a0a0a → #111111, so the field was painted the same colour as the thing
       behind it and disappeared entirely. A tint of the text colour reads as raised over the
       gradient AND over the rays, wherever in the band it happens to sit. */
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 7%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 14%, transparent);
  }

  .sh-field:hover,
  .sh-field:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
  }

  .sh-field-icon {
    display: flex;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    font-size: 20px;
  }

  .sh-field-text {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    text-align: start;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .sh-kbd {
    flex: none;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 18%, transparent);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── The questions people arrive with ── */
  .sh-chips {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .sh-chips a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 6px 12px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 14%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--bo-base-content) 6%, transparent);
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .sh-chips a:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  @media (max-width: 50rem) {
    .sh-inner { padding-block: 48px 56px; }
    .sh-title { font-size: var(--sl-text-3xl); }
  }
</style>
